CORE
JmlMapKeyOps
--function "JmlMapKeyOps.getExisting:(Ljava/util/HashMap;I)Ljava/lang/Object;" --jml-source src/main/java/JmlMapKeyOps.java --classpath target/classes:../../../../lib/java-models-library/target/core-models.jar --java-load-class java.lang.Integer --axiomatic-collections --no-unwinding-assertions --unwind 5
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for int-key autobox-aware HashMap pack_index.

The previous behaviour: JBMC's pack_index used pointer-
identity packing for boxed-Integer keys at the body's
HashMap.put / get site, while my JML resolver value-
semantically packed primitive int keys. The two didn't
agree, so a JML

  //@ requires m.containsKey(k);    where k is int

stored a non-null sentinel at one _kv slot, but the body's
m.get(k) read from a different slot, and the resulting
ensures \result != null assertion fired.

The fix in java_bytecode_axiomatic.cpp's
extract_boxed_primitive_value extends the pattern matcher
to also recognise the simpler shape

    cast(<sym>, Object*)        where <sym> has type Integer*

(in addition to the older

    cast(address_of(*<sym>.@java.lang.Number.@java.lang.Object), Object*)

shape). After the extension, both the body's HashMap.put /
get and the JML resolver's pack_index agree on
value-semantic packing of the underlying int.

The match is gated by a symbol-table lookup: we only fire
if the boxed-class struct definition carries a literal
`value` component. This requires core-models.jar on the
classpath plus --java-load-class java.lang.Integer (etc.)
to populate the struct definition.

Test method `getExisting` exercises the round-trip
explicitly: requires m.containsKey(k); body returns m.get(k);
ensures \\result != null. Verification SUCCESSFUL.
